<section class="card">
  <h2 style="margin-top:0">Contribution guidelines</h2>
  <p>DUE thrives on iterative essays. Follow these quick steps to keep contributions smooth and consistent.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plan your work:</strong> use the issue templates below to propose a new essay or suggest improvements.</li>
    <li><strong>Draft locally:</strong> copy a draft into <code>site/essays/drafts/</code>, update the front matter, then run <code>npm run start</code> for live previews.</li>
    <li><strong>Submit changes:</strong> open a pull request with a clear summary. Keep essays within their declared <code>word_range</code> and stick to ISO dates.</li>
    <li><strong>Label intent:</strong> add the <code>minor</code> label for light edits or <code>major</code> for substantial revisions before merging.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2>Credit policy</h2>
  <p>Every merged pull request updates both the version and the human credits so contributors get visible recognition.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minor contributions</strong> (labelled <code>minor</code>) trigger a patch bump and add the author to the essay's acknowledgments.</li>
    <li><strong>Major contributions</strong> (labelled <code>major</code>) advance the major version and list the contributor as a coauthor.</li>
    <li>Release notes in front matter record what changed so readers can trace each revision.</li>
    <li>Automation handles the bookkeeping—just apply the right label before merge.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2>Issue templates</h2>
  <p>Contribution flows begin from published essays. Open any essay and use the per-page buttons to propose a minor or major change.</p>
  <p>
    If you need to discuss a new topic or coordinate larger plans, start with the essay library and pick a place to contribute:
    <a class="inline-link" href="{{ '/essays/' | url }}">browse published essays →</a>
  </p>
</section>

